{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_es4emlds65v5qvssf4bwmrek4fi7d4hh6ueqrx6jip3ehhat4tsa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 17 tests pass. Here's a summary of what was done:\n\n## Summary\n\n### Phase 1: Translation\nImplemented `src/amp.rs` to mirror the C AMP protocol:\n\n- **`AMP_VERSION`** constant (1) — already declared.\n- **`amp_encode(argv: &[&str]) -> String`** — Builds the wire format: a header byte (`AMP_VERSION << 4 | argc`), followed by each argument as a 4-byte big-endian length + raw bytes.\n- **`Amp::decode(&mut self, buf)`** — Splits header byte: `version = high nibble`, `argc = low nibble`, and stores the remaining bytes in `self.buf` as the cursor.\n- **`Amp::decode_arg(&mut self) -> &str`** — Reads big-endian u32 length, advances the cursor, and returns the argument as a `&str`. Implementation re-arranges `self.buf` so the just-decoded argument lives at the tail (returning a `&str` borrow) while the cursor's remaining bytes precede it. This faithfully matches the cursor advance behavior in C.\n\nNo `unsafe`, no FFI, no external crates needed.\n\n### Phase 2: Tests\nCreated `src/bin/test_amp.rs` with **17 tests** covering every public API:\n- `AMP_VERSION` constant\n- `amp_encode` for: 3 args, 1 arg, empty arg interleaved, 2 args, long arg, 4 args, and **edge case argc=0**\n- `Amp::decode` header decoding (3 args, 1 arg, 0 args)\n- `Amp::decode_arg` for all the cases above (including empty string arg, long arg, multiple args)\n- A round-trip test\n\nExpected byte sequences for every encode test were generated by **running** an instrumented C program against `amp_encode`/`amp_decode`, not by reading the C source."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":405,"cache_read_input_tokens":27864,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":405,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_ti